WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.680
I kind of say that I regret doing three
languages at the same time, but if I had

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.110
the discipline, I would focus on one now.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:13.170
Hi, Steve Kaufman here and, uh, today
I want to talk about my experience

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:17.520
in learning or trying to learn
several languages at the same time.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.209
A subject I've talked about before.

00:00:20.049 --> 00:00:24.130
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, uh, click

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.630
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:26.484 --> 00:00:29.784
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:30.834 --> 00:00:34.705
So I've talked about this before, and
this is something that comes up a lot.

00:00:35.394 --> 00:00:39.684
People who do learn several languages
at the same time, or would like

00:00:39.684 --> 00:00:43.375
to learn several languages at
the same time, is it a good idea?

00:00:43.405 --> 00:00:44.334
Not a good idea?

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:44.875
And so forth.

00:00:45.565 --> 00:00:49.524
So I have tended to say it's
best to focus on one language.

00:00:50.215 --> 00:00:55.435
However, there are advantages to trying to
learn more than one language at one time.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.700
And basically that's sort of the
experiment that I embarked on.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.870
It's gotta be three years ago where
I said, I'm going to learn three

00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:08.950
Middle Eastern languages, Turkish,
Arabic, and Persian or Farsi.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:15.360
Um, and my reason for doing that was,
first of all, the reasons were one:

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.020
I didn't want to go...

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.140
I know it takes a long time
to learn any one language.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.590
So if I were to focus on Arabic
then for two years, I would

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.580
not do any Persian or Turkish.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.895
So, if I were to learn all three
languages, it might take me six

00:01:30.895 --> 00:01:35.065
years or more to achieve any kind
of a level in any one of them.

00:01:36.294 --> 00:01:41.425
So I said, well, if I go three months on
one and three months on the other, you

00:01:41.425 --> 00:01:43.405
know, I kinda maintain all three of them.

00:01:43.405 --> 00:01:47.964
I won't move along as quickly,
but I won't forget as much.

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:51.685
And so that's kind of what I've
been doing for the last three years.

00:01:53.115 --> 00:02:00.315
Uh, I decided to stop the Turkish because
one of the obstacles for me in Persian and

00:02:00.315 --> 00:02:02.145
in Arabic was simply the writing system.

00:02:02.535 --> 00:02:06.104
The problem, the difficulty in learning
a writing system is not so much

00:02:06.404 --> 00:02:10.334
learning what the different symbols
or characters or letters represent,

00:02:10.725 --> 00:02:16.575
it's actually training your brain to
read, uh, and to convert, you know,

00:02:17.829 --> 00:02:22.269
Writing into meaning instantly the
way we do in our own writing system.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.459
And it's just an awful lot harder.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.230
Uh, I, I still find it easier to read
in Czech or Polish than in Russian or

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.760
Ukrainian, even though I speak Russian,
certainly a lot better than Czech.

00:02:34.359 --> 00:02:39.100
Uh, you can't sort of discount the
sort of sense of familiarity with

00:02:39.339 --> 00:02:40.870
a script that we grew up with.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.480
And, uh, of course, you know, all of
the Cyrillic script is essentially

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.310
parallel to the Latin script.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.780
So it's not that difficult to learn.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.630
Uh, on the other hand, Persian
and Arabic present me...

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.140
at least they were difficult for me.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.780
So I said, okay, I'm going to focus
on them and leave the Turkish.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.430
So I've been doing that.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.990
And I would say that really
my progress has suffered.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.150
Uh, I have, uh, a smattering of
certainly Arabic and Persian.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:16.250
Um, not only that in Arabic, I've
decided to expose myself to Levantine

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:21.820
Arabic and, uh, Egyptian Arabic, which
also introduces a level of confusion.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.230
But, um, and the reason I'm doing that
is that I want to be able to understand

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:32.409
movies from Lebanon, movies from Egypt
and, um, Arabic speaking people, they

00:03:32.409 --> 00:03:36.640
speak their own dialect to each other
and they somehow manage to communicate

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.900
across different dialects with the
exception of people from North Africa.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.150
So I kind of feel if I'm doing my
Arabic thing, then I should kind of

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.800
be familiar with Levantine Arabic and
Egyptian Arabic, and actually the two

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:55.810
Egyptisn and Levantine are closer to each
other than they are to Standard Arabic.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.330
But you still need the Standard Arabic
in order to read and in order to

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:04.330
understand, you know, radio political
podcasts and things of that nature.

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:09.204
So my Arabic is kind of at a standstill.

00:04:09.774 --> 00:04:14.424
Uh, Persian I think I'm doing
better at, and I have, it's more

00:04:14.424 --> 00:04:17.784
comfortable, easier for me to speak
in Persian when I speak to my tutor.

00:04:19.044 --> 00:04:22.914
But there's no question that if
I were to focus on one, I would

00:04:22.914 --> 00:04:24.505
be much, much further ahead.

00:04:24.984 --> 00:04:30.685
And so then I say to myself, okay, why
don't I just focus on Persian now and

00:04:30.685 --> 00:04:32.445
trying to get it up to a decent level?

00:04:33.295 --> 00:04:37.075
Uh, but then of course there's always
that, geez, you know, I come across an

00:04:37.075 --> 00:04:39.445
interesting something in Levantine Arabic.

00:04:39.505 --> 00:04:44.755
Uh, in fact, I bought a book
recently, um, on, uh, Levantine Arabic

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:46.914
grammar and Levantine Arabic verbs.

00:04:46.914 --> 00:04:49.164
And it's fun going to
the lead through that.

00:04:49.164 --> 00:04:53.094
So it's kind of hard to let
go, but I probably should.

00:04:53.215 --> 00:04:57.354
And no sooner have I done that than
I, I was scheduled to have a, uh,

00:04:57.385 --> 00:04:58.945
an interview or a conversation.

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:03.520
Uh, a YouTuber who speaks
Japanese and Turkish.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.560
So I said, geez, I'd better
brush up on my Turkish.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.630
So I go back to my Turkish and
after an initial three months

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:16.000
focused on Turkish, I did have an
exit video as I call it in Turkish,

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.830
where I actually was able to speak.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:19.210
I couldn't do that now.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.840
Uh, but then I go back to Turkish,
go back to the mini stories,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.340
go back to a bunch of stuff.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.380
And I realized it was how
much I have forgotten.

00:05:27.205 --> 00:05:30.745
And also how many words
there are for me to learn.

00:05:31.495 --> 00:05:36.295
So I'm kinda nowhere in Turkish,
uh, not where I would like to be in

00:05:36.295 --> 00:05:40.885
Arabic a little closer, where I, to,
where I would like to be in Persian.

00:05:41.005 --> 00:05:44.725
And now it turns out that I have
an interview or a discussion

00:05:44.725 --> 00:05:46.795
with someone from Korea.

00:05:47.455 --> 00:05:48.655
And so I said, you know what?

00:05:48.665 --> 00:05:50.305
I should kind of brush up my Korean.

00:05:50.315 --> 00:05:54.635
I've spent a lot of time on Korean
over the years on again off again.

00:05:55.135 --> 00:05:57.505
And it's really not at a
level that I would like.

00:05:58.465 --> 00:06:03.355
I'm sure if I, if, you know, when
I have been in Korea can have sort

00:06:03.355 --> 00:06:04.825
of casual conversation with people.

00:06:04.825 --> 00:06:09.295
I do have theoretically, a large passive
vocabulary, but in fact, I struggle.

00:06:10.015 --> 00:06:14.875
Um, so I've been doing very Korean
plus I'm down here in Palm Springs.

00:06:14.875 --> 00:06:19.855
Again, you can see my fake bookshop
behind me and we're at a golf course

00:06:19.855 --> 00:06:25.195
and there's a large group of young
Koreans who are, uh, this is sort of like

00:06:25.195 --> 00:06:27.735
their golf camp with their instructors.

00:06:27.735 --> 00:06:32.635
So I like to go and chat a bit and,
uh, and, uh, watch them, with them

00:06:32.635 --> 00:06:34.104
all and see if I been imitate them.

00:06:34.674 --> 00:06:35.755
So the Korean is back.

00:06:35.755 --> 00:06:38.065
So like, there's just no end.

00:06:38.065 --> 00:06:42.085
And so then the question really is, is
it really worthwhile doing all of this?

00:06:42.085 --> 00:06:42.835
Why bother?

00:06:43.435 --> 00:06:49.090
And so I think the question of whether
you should learn more than one language at

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:54.970
the same time, or even as a polyglot, even
if you're not doing it at the same time,

00:06:55.240 --> 00:07:00.940
you are entertaining, you are balancing
like this, this, uh, you know, a juggler

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.950
who's got all these balls in the air.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:08.470
Uh, obviously you can't maintain
them all, you know, you're

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:09.820
catching stuff that's falling down.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:12.400
You can't maintain them all
at the same at the same time.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:21.700
Um, so then you, I think it very much
depends on the, the sort of, uh, you know,

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:23.950
what your needs are at any given time.

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:28.690
And I definitely enjoy exploring
languages, even the little

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.100
bit of Turkish that I have.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.710
I'm very happy that I, I I've done that.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:36.850
Uh, I'm happy because I enjoy when
I go back to Turkey and I hear the

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:41.710
sounds of Turkish, uh, when I watch
a series in Turkish I get a bit of a

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:44.619
sense of what they're talking about
or the odd word here and there.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.080
And I'm very happy that
I, that I did that.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:50.859
I'm very happy that I did what I
did in Korean, even though it's not

00:07:50.859 --> 00:07:52.359
quite where I would like it to be.

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:57.400
So I think that the simple answer is,
you know, should you study more than

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.179
one language at the same time, or even,
should you study more than one language?

00:08:01.239 --> 00:08:04.239
Should you focus on one language
and try to be as good as you

00:08:04.239 --> 00:08:06.109
possibly can be in that language?

00:08:06.539 --> 00:08:07.319
That's...

00:08:08.059 --> 00:08:10.854
Finally, you know, in the
end, that's up to you.

00:08:11.414 --> 00:08:16.705
I very much applaud people who want
to become as good as they can be in

00:08:16.705 --> 00:08:23.094
language X, but at the same time,
uh, it's such a wonderful thing.

00:08:23.094 --> 00:08:30.354
And I think back to that great
gentle giant Moses McCormick who

00:08:31.255 --> 00:08:33.985
tragically died at a very young age.

00:08:33.985 --> 00:08:39.580
Whose life was spent exploring all
kinds of unconnected languages from

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:45.010
every corner of the world and probably
not achieving, you know, C2 or

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:49.330
anything in any of those languages,
but certainly capable of handling

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.370
conversations in a variety of languages.

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:52.560
So it depends what you want to do.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.820
Do you want to explore, or you
want to be a perfectionist?

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:58.480
Uh, but if you want to do
well in the language, then I

00:08:58.480 --> 00:08:59.990
certainly think you have to focus.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:06.010
And, uh, I kinda say that I regret doing
three languages at the same time, but if I

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:08.380
had the discipline, I would focus on one.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:13.600
Now I would go and focus on Persian
and get it up to a really good level

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:19.840
and then go back in and pick up the
pieces, uh, of my Arabic or, or.

00:09:21.185 --> 00:09:25.135
And, uh, I've still got pieces
to pick up in a Korean and

00:09:25.135 --> 00:09:27.035
Czech and Greek and Romanian.

00:09:27.035 --> 00:09:30.445
So that in a sense,
comes with a territory.

00:09:30.655 --> 00:09:34.885
If you're going to be a polyglot and
enjoy exploring languages, you're

00:09:34.885 --> 00:09:39.025
never going to be satisfied, uh,
with your level in any of them,

00:09:39.445 --> 00:09:41.545
but you're going to enjoy them all.

00:09:41.875 --> 00:09:46.195
So just a bit of a ramble,
uh, on the subject of studying

00:09:46.195 --> 00:09:47.845
more than online at the same.

00:09:48.775 --> 00:09:49.675
Thank you for listening.

00:09:49.705 --> 00:09:51.265
I hope you find that useful.

00:09:51.505 --> 00:09:57.385
And I think I'll leave you my exit videos
after three months of Persian and three

00:09:57.385 --> 00:10:04.075
months of Turkish and, uh, of those two, I
think I've improved in Persian since then.

00:10:04.165 --> 00:10:05.905
I haven't improved it in Turkish.

00:10:06.635 --> 00:10:07.255
Bye for now.

